1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,247
Book Two, Proposition Three.

2
00:00:02,247 --> 00:00:14,256
If a segment be cut at random, the rectangle contained by the whole and one of the segments is equal to the rectangle contained by the segments and the square on the aforesaid segment.

3
00:00:14,256 --> 00:00:17,650
Let the segment A B be cut at random at the point C.

4
00:00:18,070 --> 00:00:22,740
On C B, describe the square C D E B by One Forty-Six.

5
00:00:22,740 --> 00:00:32,080
Extend E D through to F by Postulate Two, and through A draw A F parallel to C D or B E by One Thirty-One.

6
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:45,171
Then the whole rectangle A E equals the rectangle A D together with the square C E; and because B E equals B C, A E is the rectangle contained by A B and B C.

7
00:00:45,171 --> 00:00:53,190
Because D C equals C B, A D is the rectangle contained by A C and C B; and D B is the square on C B.

8
00:00:53,190 --> 00:01:03,456
Therefore, the rectangle contained by A B and B C equals the rectangle contained by A C and C B together with the square on B C.

9
00:01:03,456 --> 00:01:05,300
Which was to be proved.

10
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:12,516
In algebraic terms, if a segment is cut into a and b, the whole a plus b multiplied by a equals a squared plus a b.

11
00:01:12,516 --> 00:01:27,528
As Heath notes, this proposition was constantly cited by Heron and Pappus, and when combined with Propositions One and Two, it immediately proves that the difference of two squares equals the rectangle contained by the sum and difference of the segments.

12
00:01:27,528 --> 00:01:38,520
